[common]
display_name="Appliance Compositor"
desc="Shared display and sound server for screen apps (a front panel, a TV app): one Wayland compositor (weston + appliance-shell) on every display, and one PipeWire sound server. No window of its own; other apps depend on it."
changelog="1.2.0: touchscreens now act on their own display. An external touch monitor plugged in next to a built-in touch panel used to steer the built-in screen; the compositor now pairs each touchscreen with its display by itself, remembers the pairing, and apps or the administrator can set it explicitly ([touchscreen] in clients.d). 1.1.1: after a reboot the screens no longer stay dark (the compositor started under the boot splash and could not take the displays); it now waits for the splash to end. 1.1.0: keyboard focus by priority: an app can keep the keyboard and remote while it runs (rule key focus-priority), so a TV app keeps the remote even when the front panel's screensaver is up on the other screen. Windows can move to another screen without restarting the compositor (control command set-output), and move to their screen when it is connected later. A window whose screen is not connected can stay hidden instead of opening on another app's screen (rule key output-fallback=none). 1.0.0: first release. weston 14 with appliance-shell (window stacking by rule, per-window rotation, control socket) and the appliance-screensaver, taken over from T6 Front Panel; PipeWire + WirePlumber + pipewire-pulse run system-wide. Apps register their windows and outputs in /etc/appliance-compositor/clients.d."
